// Linux clone(2) (syscall-first; no libpthread).
//
// Signature matches the kernel ABI:
//   clone(flags, child_stack, ptid, ctid, tls) -> tid (parent) / 0 (child) / -errno
//
// Notes:
// - `ctid` is used for CLONE_CHILD_SETTID / CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID (thread join via futex).
// - `ptid` / `tls` are optional for v0 bring-up; callers may pass 0.
fn sys_clone(flags, stack, ptid, ctid, tls) { return 0 }
// Linux syscall-first OS thread create (clone wrapper; v0).
//
// This is a compiler intrinsic (lowered by the native backends) that:
// - creates a new OS thread via clone(2) (no libpthread),
// - runs `start_addr(arg_ptr)` inside the child,
// - terminates the child thread with `sys_exit(0)` (must never return to the caller frame).
//
// Signature:
//   sys_thread_create(start_addr, arg_ptr, stack_top, ctid_ptr) -> tid (parent) or -errno
//
// Notes:
// - `stack_top` must point to writable stack memory (typically an mmap-backed stack mapping end).
// - `ctid_ptr` must point to a writable 4-byte slot for CLONE_CHILD_SETTID/CLEARTID.
fn sys_thread_create(start_addr, arg_ptr, stack_top, ctid_ptr) { return 0 }
